## Cut: 2 problems, not 5

p1 = Q1 + Q2 + Q3 + Q5 (24 pts).  Merged, deliberately:
  (a) HARD DEPENDENCY: Q5's own baseline cell (notebook cell 30) trains *MyGCNConv*
      from Q1 at 2 and 8 layers. Q5's whole argument is "this GCN over-smooths, mine
      does not". Split them and two different GCNs exist and the comparison is void.
  (b) SHARED NONDETERMINISTIC ARTEFACT: all four consume the same node2vec matrix X
      (cell 7). gensim's Word2Vec is run with workers=4, so it is NOT reproducible
      across processes even at a fixed seed. Two workers => two different X => the
      GCN / GAT / GIN / DeepConv MRRs land in one table while having been measured
      against different inputs. That is precisely the "two workers build the same
      object two different ways" failure.
  (c) Same harness (train_and_eval, cell 12), same graph, same splits, and the
      notebook requires the GraphSAGE demo to be run once first as the control.
  The four are instances of one exercise over one fixed scaffold, so the marginal cost
  of 2nd..4th after the environment is up is small.

p2 = Q4 (6 pts).  Kept separate: it shares nothing expensive or nondeterministic with
  p1. Different graph (load_hetero, 3 relations), different features (metapath2vec
  X_HETERO, cell 26 -- does NOT use node2vec X), different wrapper
  (RGCNLinkPredictor) and different loop (train_and_eval_hetero). Only load_social()
  (deterministic) and evaluate_ranking() are common, so there is no way for the two
  workers to contradict each other on a number.

