## 13:45-13:58  p2: FAIL round 1, PASS round 2

Its validator earned its keep. The worker's answer asserted that pooled c_{i,r} helps
because averaging inside the two ~400-degree listening relations "smooths the users
flat". The validator ran a 4-variant x 3-seed ablation of its own and showed the stated
mechanism predicts the WRONG SIGN: pooling rescales listens by x1.00 and listened_by by
x0.98 (i.e. barely at all) while dividing the sparse `friend` term by 22, and pooling
`friend` ALONE recovers more than the whole gain (0.329 vs 0.296 pooled-all vs 0.190
control). Round 2 replaced the claim with the one the data supports. That is exactly the
class of defect -- a self-consistent, confidently-worded, wrong explanation -- that
survives a worker's own review, and it was caught by measurement, not by argument.

